glimt: rate limits and storage ceilings

Task 311, PRD 019 §8 and §11 Q8.

The byte budget needed a new type. ratelimit.Limiter answers "how many times", which is
right for a PIN request and wrong for an upload: sixty thumbnails and sixty 12 MiB videos
are the same number of events and nowhere near the same cost. ratelimit.Budget is a
per-key sliding-window sum, kept separate from Limiter rather than widening a signature a
dozen callers use honestly. A refused charge is not recorded, so a member refused a large
video can still post the small photo they settle for; an oversized charge is refused
rather than clamped, since clamping would let one through per window and read as the limit
not working.

The read limiter is the point of the task, and the test that matters asserts that nothing
is throttled. 600/minute per member, two orders of magnitude looser than the writes and
measured over a shorter window. PRD 019 §0a.3 describes the post-race browse as the load
peak of the feature -- a thousand people at the finish line pulling a grid of thumbnails
per screen -- and it is the use the feature was built for. A limiter near the upload
numbers would refuse the fourth screen, and an hourly read budget would be spent by
somebody scrolling for two minutes and then lock them out for fifty-eight. The test drives
200 reads through the real handler with the production numbers wired in, including the
write limiters, so if reads and writes ever share a limiter again that is what fails. This
is a trap rather than an optimisation: a throttled browse does not look like a bug from
the server's side, it looks like a working rate limiter.

Making the limits configurable exposed a live bug. Limiter.Allow refuses when
len(kept) >= limit, so New(0, w) blocks every request. No existing caller passes 0, so it
had never mattered -- but with the documented "zero means unlimited" convention,
GLIMT_PER_HOUR=0 would have taken the whole feature down silently while looking like a
working limit. limiterOrNil fixes it at the call site rather than changing ratelimit.New,
whose other callers are fine and whose reading is defensible on its own terms.

§11 Q8 is answered: reject, never evict. Eviction would mean this feature's one
irreversible operation firing with no human involved and nobody told, deleting one
member's memories to make room for another's. Retention is what frees space, on a schedule
everybody was told about.

Two ceilings with two different status codes, because the difference decides what the
client tells the member. Per member is 429 -- their quota, and they can act on it, so the
message says to delete something. Total is 507: the server is out of room, nothing the
member did wrong and nothing they can do, so a 429 would be a lie and "try again" invites
a loop. The check fails open, the opposite of how the moderation check fails, and
deliberately: a ceiling is a safety margin, not an authorization, and the cost of wrongly
refusing is a member in a forest losing a photograph they cannot retake.

The storage sums overcount on purpose. Content addressing means identical bytes are one
object, so summing glimt_media.bytes exceeds real disk use when media are shared. As a
per-member quota that is arguably more correct than the disk figure -- a member's budget
should reflect what they posted, not whether a stranger posted the same file first -- and
as an input to the total ceiling it errs towards refusing early, which is the safe
direction for a volume that cannot be replayed. Hidden glimt are counted, since hiding
does not delete. The query shape is asserted off the text, including the COALESCE, without
which a member who has posted nothing would produce a scan error.

The byte figures are guesses, like the retention defaults, and should be reviewed before
the event. None of this has been exercised under real load; task 324 is the load test and
is what would tell us whether 600 reads/minute is right.

The task 312 annotation guard immediately caught the new 507 branch as undocumented, which
is what it was built for.
